00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:32.449
We here at Journey to the Microcosmos have
been very excited for everyone who has ordered

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:39.410
their very own Journey to the Microcosmos
microscope and started their own explorations.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:45.610
And we love seeing the photos and videos you’ve
shared with us on Twitter of all of the rotifers

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.850
and nematodes that you’ve been finding already.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:54.989
Back before these microscopes were available
to buy though, my trusty co-host Hank took

00:00:54.989 --> 00:01:02.449
a demo out for a spin and saw a lot of…this
floating around in the water.

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:08.350
And as any of us would do if we had an expert
in our contacts, Hank decided to turn to our

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:15.039
master of microscopes James for help identifying
these hairy lima beans.

00:01:15.039 --> 00:01:21.689
And James replied with the following warning:
“ they are ostracods and they will eat everything

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:27.670
in your sample and poop all the time, until
there is nothing left in your sample but a

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:34.439
bunch of ostracods swimming in their poop.”

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:41.359
Ostracods are a crustacean, encompassing tens
of thousands of species and whose bodies mostly

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:44.852
range from around 0.2 to 2 millimeters in
length.

00:01:44.852 --> 00:01:51.450
And thankfully, their size makes them large
enough for James to filter out using a simple

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:58.799
tea infuser, which is how he prevents these
animals from turning his samples into a toilet.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:03.390
But their size is not just a useful feature
for filtering.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:10.369
It’s also a clue into a much more fundamental
question about the trade-offs between sexual

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:15.519
reproduction and survival.

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:20.390
To explain what that means, we need to establish
two things.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:25.480
The first is that if you’re looking at a
male ostracod, you are essentially looking

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.180
at a crawling shelled penis.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:36.719
Do you remember how we said earlier that the
ostracod usually range from around .2-2 mm in length?

00:02:36.719 --> 00:02:43.700
Well, for male ostracods, about a third of
that length is made up of penis—or rather

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:52.709
two hemipenes, which make up what ostracod
scientists have called “the large, muscular sperm pump.”

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:58.920
The two hemipenes in the males correspond
to the two openings on the female ostracod,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.359
which hold several dozen eggs ready for fertilization.

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:09.019
And these eggs can have different fathers,
so male ostracods compete with each other

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:15.859
in part by having their large, muscular sperm
pump actually pump out large sperm.

00:03:15.859 --> 00:03:23.270
And when I say “large,” I don’t just
mean “large for such a tiny animal.”

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:29.080
Ostracods produce some of the largest sperm
in the animal kingdom...sometimes up to 10

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:36.689
millimeters in length, which is around 182
times longer than human sperm.

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:43.299
The second thing that we need to establish
is that ostracods are remarkably well-preserved.

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:48.849
Their bodies are protected by two shells connected
at a hinge, which shelters their soft bodies

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:53.040
in life and secures their remains in death.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.819
There are ostracod fossils dating back at
least 450 million years.

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:04.189
And in their many hundreds of millions of
years, they have spread to so many waters

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:10.040
and been so well-preserved that their fossils
tell us stories of how the world’s climate

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:17.299
has changed over that long period and how
this little crustacean has adapted in response.

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:26.930
But in addition to their geological storytelling,
ostracod fossils have a few other very notable distinctions.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:33.699
One distinction is that the oldest fossilized
animal penis we have found belongs to an ostracod,

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:38.110
one that existed around 425 million years
ago.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:45.090
The species was named Colymbosathon ecplecticos,
which translates to “astounding swimmer

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:50.639
with a large penis.”
Another distinction is that the oldest fossilized

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:56.280
sperm belongs to—yes, you guessed it—the
ostracod.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.240
The remains were found in an Australian bat
cave, where the phosphorus from many years

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:08.840
worth of bat poop ensured that the soft insides
of the ostracod would remain frozen in time.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.610
Scientists even found a female ostracod whose
remains held post-coital sperm.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:20.389
And these simple facts about the ostracod
may paint a somewhat absurd image of ancient

00:05:20.389 --> 00:05:22.800
crustacean genitalia.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:31.160
But within that absurd image is also a profound
question: is it worth it?

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:38.699
Is it worth the exceptional amount of space
and energy that the male ostracod devotes to reproduction?

00:05:38.699 --> 00:05:43.580
To ensuring that its sperm will outcompete
all others?

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:49.110
The animal kingdom is full of examples of
this seemingly simple question.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:56.819
Think of the peacock, whose ornate train is
exquisitely crafted to attract the comparatively drab peahen.

00:05:56.819 --> 00:06:02.800
The ostracod’s equivalent is its size: the
longer the male ostracod, the more it has

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.140
invested in its reproductive needs.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:10.930
And in general male ostracods are longer than
their female counterparts.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:16.810
The accumulation of these kinds of differences
is called sexual dimorphism.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:23.230
The higher the degree of dimorphism, the more
different the male and female of a species are.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:27.509
Some animals have high dimorphism, some have
low.

00:06:27.509 --> 00:06:40.840
And so on both ends, the question remains:
is it worth it?

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:46.520
On the one hand, for species with high sexual
dimorphism, the energy an individual might

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:53.040
spend ensuring it stands out from other potential
mates is energy that goes towards a biological

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:57.640
legacy, one that hopefully aids the species
overall.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:03.530
But that is also energy that potentially comes
at the cost of other essential elements of

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:10.820
an animal’s survival or makes them vulnerable,
leading eventually to their extinction.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:16.500
It can be difficult to untangle this balance
with animals we see today, whose present day

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:22.900
survival at the very least means their choices
haven’t led to extinction…yet.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.030
But consider the ostracod.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:34.030
This is a remarkably well-preserved animal with a long history of investment in sexual reproduction.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:40.039
And over that long history, many species of
ostracod have gone extinct, their beginnings

00:07:40.039 --> 00:07:44.240
and ends documented in the fossil record.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.580
So in 2018, researchers studied 93 extinct
ostracod species that lived in the Mississippi

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:55.360
area around 66-84 million years ago.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.069
They turned to the fossil record to quantify
the differences in size and shape between

00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:05.580
male and female ostracods of each species,
as well as to measure how long it took for

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.449
each species to go extinct.

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:13.189
They found that when males were larger than
females—meaning that they invested more

00:08:13.189 --> 00:08:19.580
energy in sexual reproduction—those species
tended to go extinct 10 times faster than

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.620
species where the males were smaller.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:28.139
We’re talking about the ability to survive
as a species for 1.6 million years versus

00:08:28.139 --> 00:08:31.189
15.5 million years.

00:08:31.189 --> 00:08:36.700
So when it comes to the original question
“is it worth it?” this result seems like

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:38.720
a resounding “no.”

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:44.530
For the ostracod, the compulsion to invest
in sexual reproduction at the individual level

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:48.440
seems to come at a cost to the species overall.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:55.330
But this is one very specific context, with
one very specific animal and its one very

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.700
specific sexual dimorphism.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:04.690
The story of survival and reproduction is
more than just any one animal, and the question

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:18.370
of “is it worth it?” has only living answers
told across past, present, and future.

